<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Locri Epizephyrii</h1><p class="st-meta">680 BCE – 205 BCE (approx.) · capital Locri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek city in Calabria famed for the law code of Zaleucus, the earliest written Greek laws, and for its sanctuary of Persephone.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a> from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> founded Locri Epizephyrii, 'Locri of the West Wind', around 680 BCE on the Ionian coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, near Cape Zephyrion. Ancient writers said the settlers included slaves and women of noble families, a story that may reflect the status of women in the city's aristocracy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Locri's laws, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaleucus">Zaleucus</a> in the mid-7th century BCE, were reckoned the first written code in the Greek world and known for severe punishments. A council of a thousand governed the city. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a> sanctuary yielded hundreds of painted terracotta plaques (pinakes). Locri defeated much larger <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/croton">Croton</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-sagra">Sagras</a> river and founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hipponion">Hipponion</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medma">Medma</a>. It was Syracuse's closest ally: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> married a Locrian woman, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-ii-of-syracuse">Dionysius II</a> ruled the city harshly from 356 to 346 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Locri became a Roman ally after the Pyrrhic War, went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and was retaken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio</a> in 205 BCE; the outrages of its commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-pleminius">Pleminius</a> caused a scandal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It continued as a Roman town until abandoned in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/locri-epizephyrii.jpg" alt="Locri Epizephyrii" width="320" height="326"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manifattura_locrese,_pinax_votiva_con_hermes_e_afrodite,_470-460_ac_ca._(reggio_calabria,_man).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>680 BCE – 205 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Locri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dionysius II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Locri, Lokroi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-442&amp;focus=locri-epizephyrii" aria-label="Locri Epizephyrii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5085 804 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Locri Epizephyrii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5405 1060L5383 1099M5405 1060L5304 942M5405 1060L5400 1046"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 1099h0M5304 942h0M5400 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5405 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5405 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-442&amp;focus=locri-epizephyrii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

